{"conjecture": "Long-running software systems degrade because developers optimize for local correctness—passing tests and minimizing immediate risk—while accumulating an implicit dependency graph of behavioral assumptions that are never captured in types or interfaces. Over time, the ratio of these hidden, unobservable couplings to explicit couplings grows, making the blast radius of any change computationally intractable to predict from the code alone. Thus, maintenance difficulty increases because the system's true constraint surface is an emergent property of historical interactions, not a reducible artifact of its current structure."}
